            <p>If you have many product photos, social media images, or scanned documents, compressing them individually is tedious. Our tool lets you compress <strong>up to 50 images at once</strong> and download them as a ZIP file – all in your browser, private and fast.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>✅ <strong>100% client‑side</strong> – no upload, no server, complete privacy.</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Preserves original resolution</strong> – we never resize your images.</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Adjustable quality</strong> – control file size vs. image quality.</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Multiple formats</strong> – JPEG, PNG, WebP output.</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Download as ZIP</strong> – get all compressed images in one archive.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">🔄 How It Works</h2>
            <ol>
                <li>Select multiple images (drag & drop or click).</li>
                <li>Choose output format (JPEG, PNG, or WebP) and quality (for JPEG/WebP).</li>
                <li>Click “Compress All Images” – we process each image locally using Canvas.</li>
                <li>Review the file size reduction for each image.</li>
                <li>Click “Download ZIP” to save all compressed images.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>All processing uses the browser’s native image encoding – no external libraries except JSZip for ZIP creation.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert alert-info border-0 rounded-4 mt-3">
        <h5 class="alert-heading">💡 Compression Tips</h5>
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li><strong>JPEG</strong> is best for photographs – reduce quality to 70–85% for good balance.</li>
            <li><strong>PNG</strong> is lossless – good for logos, text, but file sizes are larger.</li>
            <li><strong>WebP</strong> offers excellent compression; set quality similar to JPEG.</li>
            <li>Original dimensions are always preserved – no unwanted resizing.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

